{"kind":"report","text":"Adopted as proposed. For opt-in scopes only, creation requires the complete reachable child-definition closure at birth. If any declaration is missing, creation is refused before genesis, with a validation problem that names the missing definition. This uses the existing creation and validation path: it adds no act refusal reason and no new recorded field. Replay derives that same closure at genesis with the existing create selectors and the namedDefinitions limit, and counts it there. Each definition counts once per byte domain and digest. Its bound is finite (definitionBytes times namedDefinitions), so this is not reserve-unbounded. If a definition-domain value is first retained by a later act or outcome, count it at that entry, which is where it is first stored. Older scopes and their replay are unchanged. This follows decision 63195bdd: opt-in only, never count zero, and the same derivation at runtime and in replay.","body":{"decision":"adopt: opt-in creation requires the complete reachable child-definition closure at birth","request":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b7fd029592dec8aace4e0c5f8f3d25844f1335c0","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}